<article class="env-exercise" id="028M">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/028M">Exercise <span data-tag="028M">111.32.6</span>.</a> Let $X = \mathbf{R}$ with the usual topology. Let $\mathcal{O}_ X = \underline{\mathbf{Z}/2\mathbf{Z}}_ X$. Let $i : Z = \{ 0\}  \to X$ be the inclusion and let $\mathcal{O}_ Z = \underline{\mathbf{Z}/2\mathbf{Z}}_ Z$. Prove the following (the first three follow from the definitions but if you are not clear on the definitions you should elucidate them): </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$i_*\mathcal{O}_ Z$ is a skyscraper sheaf. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
There is a canonical surjective map from $\underline{\mathbf{Z}/2\mathbf{Z}}_ X \to i_*\underline{\mathbf{Z}/2\mathbf{Z}}_ Z$. Denote the kernel $\mathcal{I} \subset \mathcal{O}_ X$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathcal{I}$ is an ideal sheaf of $\mathcal{O}_ X$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The sheaf $\mathcal{I}$ on $X$ cannot be locally generated by sections (as in Modules, Definition <a href="/tag/01B2" data-tag="01B2">17.8.1</a>.) 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>